    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and it passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the \"reject a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date \u2192 400\" rule as a small, self-contained OfficeFloor step, matching the existing reject-step pattern (`RejectOwnerCityAtCapacity`, `RejectOwnerDailyLimit`). No existing function or class was modified in its body \u2014 structural impact stayed low by adding cohesive new units rather than growing anything.\n\n**New files:**\n- `RejectFutureRegistrationDate.java` \u2014 one-method step reading `@Val Owner`, throwing when the date is in the future.\n- `FutureRegistrationDateException.java` \u2014 checked exception (400 semantics).\n- `FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `400 Bad Request` via `ProblemDetails`.\n- `...FutureRegistrationDateException.yml` \u2014 escalation wiring.\n\n**One YAML edit:** inserted `rejectFutureRegistrationDate` into the `POST /api/owners` pipeline right after `build`.\n\nOne correctness detail worth flagging: `BuildOwner` rolls the supplied date to its next business day before this step sees it, so I compare the built date against **today rolled the same way** (`BusinessDay.nextBusinessDay(LocalDate.now())`). That keeps a same-day or absent registration accepted on any weekday \u2014 including weekends, avoiding a false rejection of normal creations \u2014 while a genuinely future date (e.g. the test's `2999-01-01`) is rejected.",
